EXPERIMENT 1
Study of NMR Phenomenon and Signal Analysis
1. Aim
To understand, using an online simulator, how nuclear spins behave in a magnetic field, how an RF pulse tips them, and how the resulting Free Induction Decay (FID) signal is formed and analysed.
2. Software / Online Simulator Required
Bloch Simulator (DRCMR) — a free, browser-based Bloch-equation simulator that needs no installation or login.
Simulator link:
https://www.drcmr.dk/BlochSimulator⁠�
3. What You Should Know Before You Start
A nuclear spin placed in a static magnetic field B₀ lines up and precesses around the field direction — this precession is called Larmor precession.
An RF pulse (usually a 90-degree pulse) tips the spin away from the B₀ axis into the transverse plane.
Once tipped, the spin keeps precessing and slowly returns to its original position — this return is called relaxation, described by two time constants T1 and T2.
The signal picked up by the receiver coil while the spin relaxes is called the Free Induction Decay (FID) — it looks like a decaying, oscillating wave.
Studying the FID tells us the Larmor frequency, and the T1/T2 relaxation times of the sample.
4. Step-by-Step Procedure
Open the simulator link given above in any web browser (Chrome/Firefox works best; rotate a mobile screen to landscape).
Set a value for the static magnetic field B₀ using the on-screen slider and note the Larmor frequency shown.
Choose a starting condition for the spin (equilibrium, pointing along B₀).
Apply a 90-degree RF pulse using the pulse button and observe the spin vector tipping into the transverse plane.
Watch the free precession of the spin and note how the signal (FID) decays with time.
Change the T1 and T2 relaxation values from the menu and repeat the pulse to see how faster/slower relaxation changes the FID shape.
Record your readings in the observation table below for at least five different settings.
Sketch the FID waveform you observe for two of your trials in your record.
