EXPERIMENT 2
Create and Visualize Quantum States using a Quantum Circuit and Simulate Projective Measurements
1. Aim
To build simple single-qubit and two-qubit states on a quantum circuit simulator, visualise them, and perform projective measurements to verify the expected probabilities.
2. Software / Online Simulator Required
IBM Quantum Composer — a free drag-and-drop quantum circuit builder that runs in the browser; no software installation is needed and no login is required for up to 4 qubits.
Simulator link:
https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/composer⁠�
3. What You Should Know Before You Start
A qubit's state is written as:
|ψ⟩ = α|0⟩ + β|1⟩
where |α|² and |β|² give the probability of measuring 0 or 1.
Applying gates such as X, H (Hadamard), Y or Z changes the state of a qubit before measurement.
A Hadamard gate on |0⟩ creates an equal superposition:
(|0⟩ + |1⟩)/√2
giving 50% chance of 0 and 50% chance of 1 on measurement.
Projective measurement collapses the qubit to either |0⟩ or |1⟩ and the simulator reports outcome probabilities after many repeated shots.
The Bloch sphere and probability histogram in the Composer let you visually confirm the state you built.
4. Step-by-Step Procedure
Open the IBM Quantum Composer link given above (sign-in is optional for circuits up to 4 qubits).
Start a new circuit with one qubit, initially in state |0⟩.
Drag an X gate onto the qubit line and run the circuit — verify the state flips to |1⟩.
Reset and this time drag an H gate onto the qubit — observe the resulting superposition on the Bloch sphere/statevector view.
Add a measurement operation and run the circuit; note the probability of getting 0 and 1 from the histogram.
Repeat with a two-qubit circuit: apply H on qubit 0, then measure both qubits, and note the outcome probabilities.
Try at least four different gate combinations (X, H, Y, Z, or combinations) and record the prepared state and measured probabilities each time.
