EXPERIMENT 3
Demonstrate Quantum Circuits using Hadamard and CNOT Gates; Measure Violations of Bell Inequalities
1. Aim
To construct entangled Bell states using Hadamard and CNOT gates and to experimentally verify the violation of a Bell/CHSH inequality using an online simulator.
2. Software / Online Simulator Required
Quirk – Quantum Circuit Simulator — a free, browser-based drag-and-drop simulator that also has a ready-made Bell Inequality Test (CHSH) example circuit built in.
Simulator link:
https://algassert.com/quirk⁠�
3. What You Should Know Before You Start
Applying a Hadamard gate on qubit 0 followed by a CNOT gate (qubit 0 as control, qubit 1 as target) creates the entangled Bell state:
(|00⟩ + |11⟩)/√2
In an entangled pair, measuring one qubit instantly fixes the outcome of the other, even though each qubit alone looks random.
Classical (local, non-quantum) theories predict that a combination of correlations, called the CHSH value S, can never exceed 2.
Quantum entanglement allows this value to go up to about 2.82 (2√2), which is called violation of the Bell inequality.
Quirk's built-in 'Bell Inequality Test (CHSH)' example circuit is ready-made for this experiment — you can load it directly from the Examples menu.
4. Step-by-Step Procedure
Open the Quirk link given above.
Build a 2-qubit circuit: place an H gate on the top qubit wire, then a CNOT gate (control on top qubit, target on bottom qubit).
Add measurement to both qubits and observe that the only outcomes are 00 and 11, each with about 50% probability — this confirms entanglement.
From the Examples menu (top of page), load the ready-made 'Bell Inequality Test (CHSH)' circuit.
Note the measurement settings (angles) used for each qubit and record the correlation values shown by the simulator.
Calculate the CHSH value S from the four correlation readings using:
S = E(a,b) − E(a,b′) + E(a′,b) + E(a′,b′)
Compare your calculated S with the classical limit of 2 and note whether the inequality is violated.
