EXPERIMENT 4
Implement and Simulate the Grover Algorithm and Decoherence Algorithm
1. Aim
To implement Grover's search algorithm on a quantum circuit simulator and observe how it amplifies the probability of the correct answer; and to study the effect of decoherence (noise) on a quantum state.
2. Software / Online Simulator Required
Quirk – Quantum Circuit Simulator — the same free browser-based simulator used earlier; it has a ready-made 'Grover Search' example and also lets you add noise/measurement to observe decoherence.
Simulator link:
https://algassert.com/quirk⁠�
3. What You Should Know Before You Start
Grover's algorithm searches an unsorted list of N items in about √N steps, much faster than a classical search which needs about N/2 steps.
It works by repeatedly applying an 'oracle' (which marks the correct answer) and a 'diffusion' operator (which amplifies the marked answer's probability).
Each Grover iteration increases the probability of measuring the correct (marked) state; too many iterations can actually reduce it again, so the number of iterations matters.
Decoherence is the loss of a qubit's clean quantum behaviour due to unwanted interaction with its environment (noise), which reduces the reliability of the answer.
Simulating noise (bit-flip or phase-flip probability) on a circuit shows how the neat interference pattern of Grover's algorithm gets disturbed by decoherence.
4. Step-by-Step Procedure
Open the Quirk link and, from the Examples menu, load the ready-made 'Grover Search' circuit.
Identify the oracle stage (which marks the target item) and the diffusion stage in the loaded circuit.
Run the circuit and note the probability of the marked state after one Grover iteration.
Add one more repetition of the oracle + diffusion block and note how the probability of the correct state changes.
Continue adding iterations up to about √N and record how probability rises, and then falls if you go beyond the optimum count.
Next, on a simple 1- or 2-qubit circuit, insert a noise/depolarizing display or randomly apply an extra unwanted gate to mimic decoherence.
Compare the clean (no-noise) output probabilities with the noisy output probabilities and note the difference.
