EXPERIMENT 5
Implement and Simulate the Deutsch–Jozsa Algorithm to Distinguish Constant and Balanced Functions
1. Aim
To build the Deutsch–Jozsa circuit on a quantum simulator and use it to determine, with a single query, whether a given hidden function is constant or balanced.
2. Software / Online Simulator Required
IBM Quantum Composer — a free browser-based quantum circuit builder, used here to construct the oracle and Deutsch–Jozsa circuit manually.
Simulator link:
https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/composer⁠�
3. What You Should Know Before You Start
A function f(x) is constant if it gives the same output (all 0s or all 1s) for every input, and balanced if it gives 0 for exactly half the inputs and 1 for the other half.
Classically, distinguishing constant from balanced may need up to 2^(n-1)+1 checks in the worst case; the Deutsch–Jozsa algorithm does it with just one query using quantum parallelism.
The standard circuit: apply X then H to the ancilla (output) qubit, apply H to all input qubits, pass through the oracle for f(x), then apply H again to the input qubits and measure them.
If all input qubits measure 0, the function is constant. If any input qubit measures 1, the function is balanced.
4. Step-by-Step Procedure
Open the IBM Quantum Composer link and start a circuit with 2 qubits (1 input qubit q0, 1 ancilla qubit q1) for the simplest case.
Set the ancilla qubit q1 to |1⟩ using an X gate, then apply H gates to both q0 and q1.
Build a 'constant' oracle first — simply do nothing (identity) so f(x) = 0 for all x — then apply H to q0 again and measure q0.
Run the circuit and note that q0 measures 0 with 100% probability, confirming the function is constant.
Now change only the oracle to a 'balanced' oracle by adding a CNOT from q0 to q1, keeping everything else the same.
Apply H to q0 again, measure, and run the circuit — note that q0 now measures 1 with 100% probability, confirming the function is balanced.
Repeat with a 2-input-qubit version (3 qubits total) using a different balanced oracle to reconfirm the pattern.
